# System requirements allocate externally observable SVEMS behavior.
requirement SYS-001
title: Acquire logger observations
allocation: SVEMS
statement:
  SVEMS shall acquire each available time-based refrigerator data object from the connected logger at least once every 15 minutes.
rationale:
  The system cannot display, alarm on, or transmit observations that it has not acquired within the source recording interval.
source: WHO-EM01.2:4.2.6
decomposes: NEED-001
decomposes: NEED-004
end requirement

requirement SYS-002
title: Establish absolute event time
allocation: SVEMS
statement:
  SVEMS shall associate each downloaded logger record with UTC derived from an independently synchronized clock whose displayed time differs from server time by no more than one minute while synchronization is available.
rationale:
  The logger supplies relative time, so SVEMS must establish the absolute-time association used remotely.
source: WHO-EM01.2:4.2.1 and 4.2.4
decomposes: NEED-008
end requirement

requirement SYS-003
title: Present local monitoring state
allocation: SVEMS
statement:
  SVEMS shall present current refrigerator temperature, active alarm state, active fault state, monitoring-enabled state, and EMD energy remaining at the storage site.
rationale:
  These values let clinic personnel distinguish normal operation, excursions, faults, and intentionally disabled alarming.
source: WHO-EM01.2:4.2.9 and 4.6.1-4.6.3
decomposes: NEED-001
decomposes: NEED-009
end requirement

requirement SYS-004
title: Annunciate local alarm conditions
allocation: SVEMS
statement:
  When SVEMS detects a configured alarm condition, it shall immediately activate a locally apparent visual alarm and a 70 dB(A) or greater audible alarm measured at one metre.
rationale:
  An on-site alarm must be apparent without requiring a browser or remote network.
source: WHO-EM01.2:4.6.3
decomposes: NEED-001
end requirement

requirement SYS-005
title: Communicate remote alarm conditions
allocation: SVEMS
statement:
  SVEMS shall make a detected alarm condition known to configured remote recipients no later than 15 minutes after the alarm condition begins while an external communication path is available.
rationale:
  The delay bound connects local event detection to useful remote oversight while acknowledging an external network dependency.
source: WHO-EM01.2:4.5.10
decomposes: NEED-002
end requirement

requirement SYS-006
title: Continue essential operation without supply power
allocation: SVEMS
statement:
  Following loss of refrigerator-supplied power, SVEMS shall continue data acquisition, recording, local alarm detection, local alarm annunciation, and alarm-status communication for at least 240 hours.
rationale:
  The selected external EMD must remain useful through long refrigerator holdover periods.
source: WHO-EM01.2:4.2.12
decomposes: NEED-003
end requirement

requirement SYS-007
title: Recover from communication interruption
allocation: SVEMS
statement:
  After restoration of its configured remote communication path, SVEMS shall reconnect automatically and transmit records accumulated during the interruption without duplicating or discarding an accumulated record.
rationale:
  Local monitoring continuity is insufficient if the remote historical record remains permanently incomplete after connectivity returns.
source: WHO-EM01.2:4.5.11 + PILOT-CONTEXT:CTX-NETWORK-LOSS
decomposes: NEED-003
decomposes: NEED-004
decomposes: NEED-009
end requirement

requirement SYS-008
title: Preserve standardized cold-chain data
allocation: SVEMS
statement:
  SVEMS shall represent acquired and generated cold-chain data objects in the format defined by WHO/PQS/E006/DS01.2 and shall preserve downloaded logger files unmodified when transmitting them to the remote data service.
rationale:
  Standardized and unmodified data supports interoperability and independent downstream interpretation.
source: WHO-EM01.2:4.2.4-4.2.5
decomposes: NEED-006
end requirement

requirement SYS-009
title: Retain hosted monitoring records
allocation: SVEMS
statement:
  SVEMS shall make raw observations, alarms, faults, and derived summaries available to authorized employer users for at least three years after their recorded time.
rationale:
  The pilot chooses the standard three-year service period for operational review and investigation.
source: WHO-EM01.2:4.2.19 + CDC-PINK-5:Monitoring Vaccine Temperatures
decomposes: NEED-004
decomposes: NEED-005
end requirement

requirement SYS-010
title: Enforce authorized remote access
allocation: SVEMS
statement:
  SVEMS shall restrict remote monitoring data and administrative operations to authenticated users authorized for the applicable employer equipment.
rationale:
  Hosted monitoring should not expose clinic or equipment information to unrelated users.
source: WHO-EM01.2:4.2.22
decomposes: NEED-005
end requirement

requirement SYS-011
title: Deliver employer data externally
allocation: SVEMS
statement:
  SVEMS shall allow an authorized employer user to export all raw observations, alarms, faults, and summaries for a selected time interval in a documented machine-readable form.
rationale:
  Data ownership is operationally meaningful only if the employer can retrieve and use its information outside the hosted interface.
source: WHO-EM01.2:4.2.20-4.2.21
decomposes: NEED-005
decomposes: NEED-006
end requirement

requirement SYS-012
title: Expose monitoring-data status
allocation: SVEMS
statement:
  For each expected observation interval, SVEMS shall distinguish a received observation, an interval awaiting communication, an unavailable logger observation, and an identified monitoring fault.
rationale:
  Explicit status prevents a missing reading from being interpreted as an in-range reading.
source: CDC-PINK-5:Monitoring Vaccine Temperatures + PILOT-CONTEXT:CTX-FAULT
decomposes: NEED-009
end requirement

requirement SYS-013
title: Record configuration changes
allocation: SVEMS
statement:
  SVEMS shall record the time, authenticated actor, previous value, and new value for changes to equipment identity, time zone, alarm thresholds, alarm enablement, recipient assignments, and user authorization.
rationale:
  Investigation must distinguish equipment behavior from changes in the configuration used to interpret or communicate it.
source: PILOT-CONTEXT:Programme administrator concern
decomposes: NEED-004
decomposes: NEED-005
end requirement

requirement SYS-014
title: Avoid automated vaccine-disposition decisions
allocation: SVEMS
statement:
  SVEMS shall not classify monitored vaccines as acceptable, unusable, or releasable and shall label excursion information as input to the employer's response process.
rationale:
  The monitoring product lacks the product, exposure, and programme authority needed to make a disposition decision.
source: CDC-PINK-5:Temperature Excursions + PILOT-CONTEXT:Explicit exclusions
decomposes: NEED-010
end requirement

requirement SYS-015
title: Support field fault diagnosis
allocation: SVEMS
statement:
  SVEMS shall provide a trained technician with persistent diagnostic codes for EMD, logger-interface, power, and remote-communication faults.
rationale:
  Distinct persistent codes support restoration of monitoring without requiring the technician to infer the failed boundary.
source: WHO-EM01.2:4.2.9 and 4.9.1
decomposes: NEED-007
decomposes: NEED-009
end requirement

requirement SYS-016
title: Protect monitoring-record integrity
allocation: SVEMS
statement:
  SVEMS shall detect accidental alteration or truncation of each transferred logger file and each exported monitoring-data package.
rationale:
  Undetected transfer or export corruption could make an historical record misleading.
source: WHO-EM01.2:4.2.10 and 4.2.22
decomposes: NEED-004
decomposes: NEED-006
end requirement
